# Why We Use Bytes Instead of Percent Here

This decision was made deliberately during implementation.

Earlier,

we converted JVM Heap to a percentage because percentages provide a clearer operational view.

However,

historical graphs benefit from displaying the actual heap size.

Example

```
200 MB

↓

350 MB

↓

470 MB

↓

GC

↓

180 MB

↓

290 MB

↓

410 MB

↓

GC

↓

210 MB
```

This makes Garbage Collection behavior much easier to recognize.

---

# Understanding the Query

The metric

```promql
jvm_memory_used_bytes
```

represents the amount of heap currently occupied by Java objects.

Unlike the Gauge,

the graph displays the raw heap size rather than the percentage of available heap.

This provides greater insight into JVM behavior.

