# Expected Result

A healthy Java application typically displays a repeating saw-tooth pattern.

Example

```

500 MB

          ▲
          │
          │
450 MB    │      ▲
          │      │
400 MB    │      │
          │      │
350 MB    │      │
          │      │
300 MB    │      │
          │      │
250 MB    ▼      ▼
          │      │
200 MB ───────────────────────────────► Time

```

This pattern indicates:

- Objects are allocated.
- Heap utilization increases.
- Garbage Collection executes.
- Heap utilization decreases.
- The cycle repeats.

This is expected JVM behavior.

---

# Recognizing a Healthy JVM

Healthy applications typically exhibit:

```
Heap ↑

↓

Garbage Collection

↓

Heap ↓

↓

Heap ↑

↓

Garbage Collection

↓

Heap ↓
```

This repeating pattern indicates that the JVM is reclaiming unused objects correctly.

---

# Recognizing a Memory Leak

A memory leak appears very differently.

Example

```

150 MB

↓

220 MB

↓

300 MB

↓

420 MB

↓

520 MB

↓

640 MB

↓

780 MB

↓

900 MB

↓

OOM

```

Notice that the graph never returns to lower values.

Instead,

heap utilization continually increases.

This suggests that objects are not being released.

---

# Verification

Generate application traffic.

Refresh

```
https://api.dev.dreammyles.online/actuator/health
```

multiple times.

Observe the graph.

Heap utilization should gradually increase.

Eventually,

Garbage Collection should reduce heap utilization.

